\subsection{Router Training: A Negative Result}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The oracle margin (2.89 points) caps routing upside. The near-chance step-level accuracy admits two interpretations: (A)~episode-level labels projected onto steps create ${\sim}$88\% uninformative signal (60.3\% pre-event neutral, 27.4\% post-event), drowning learnable patterns in noise; (B)~routing signal is genuinely task-type-level---there is no step-level pattern to learn. Directional conflict is only 0.3\% of 2{,}040 training steps (labels are directionally correct in 99.7\% of informative steps), but the granularity mismatch leaves minimal signal for the router. To directly test Interpretation~A, we trained routers on death-proximal subsets---retaining only steps within $k \in \{1,3,5,10\}$ of a fatal action. If label dilution were the bottleneck, concentrating on informative steps should improve accuracy. Instead, all configurations yield internal recall of 0\% and balanced accuracy of 50\%, identical to the full-dataset baseline. The failure is structural: death-asymmetry episodes are inherently single-class (all labeled deterministic), so filtering uninformative steps cannot create internal-preferred training signal. Combined with data scaling (25\%--800\%, no improvement), death-proximal filtering and data scaling both favor Interpretation~B: routing signal is genuinely task-type-level, not step-level learnable.

An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$). At episode level, only 4B exceeds always-deterministic ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at chance. Episode-level independent evaluation yields $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$, below the majority-vote baseline of 50.0\%. Training uses episode-level splits (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}), ensuring no within-episode leakage. The high uninformative rate connects to noisy-label learning~\citep{natarajan2013learning}: standard ERM on labels with ${\sim}$88\% noise yields near-chance classifiers, consistent with our results.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} The 4B router deployed end-to-end ($N{=}245$) scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement; the $3.7\%$ internal-preferred base rate makes real-data collection expensive (Appendices~\ref{app:seed-robustness}--\ref{app:ablations}).

\section{Conclusion}

We introduced an episode-level counterfactual diagnostic framework for decomposing routing signal in modular LLM agents, overcoming per-step oracle degeneracy (97.4\% tie rate) through paired pure-strategy comparison with penalty-independent (label fraction) and penalty-dependent (DAF) metrics. Applied to ScienceWorld, 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels stem from death asymmetry---robust across four intervention axes, cross-model replication (Llama-3.1-8B, DAF${=}88.2\%$), and boundary probes (MATH, ALFWorld). The diagnostic value is practical: our 200 GPU-hour router training could not outperform the always-deterministic baseline, an outcome a 50-episode pilot would have predicted.

The framework applies to any environment with episode-level evaluation and binary catastrophic outcomes. Death-proximal labeling---concentrating training on steps near fatal actions---yields no improvement (internal recall 0\% across all $k$), confirming that step-level signal is absent in this regime. Future work should target intermediate death regimes (NetHack combat tasks) and extend to mixed-strategy routing and stronger backbones.

